import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Fundamental Learnings"
  description="High-school accessible guide to computer architecture, silicon microprocessors, memory hierarchies, and the 4 SCORE categories."
>
  <!-- Page Header -->
  <div class="page-header accent-yellow">
    <div class="badge badge-accent">START HERE &bull; ZERO TO CHIP ARCHITECT</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">Fundamental Learnings in Computer Architecture</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
      How do millions of microscopic switches carved into silicon run software, stream video, and power artificial intelligence? This guide starts from absolute first principles—designed so that <strong>high school students and newcomers</strong> can understand how computer chips work and why the <strong>4 SCORE Categories</strong> exist.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Navigation Jump -->
    <div class="primer-nav">
      <a href="#what-is-a-chip" class="primer-jump">&sect; 1. What is a Chip?</a>
      <a href="#what-is-riscv" class="primer-jump">&sect; 2. What is RISC-V?</a>
      <a href="#pipeline-laundry" class="primer-jump">&sect; 3. The Laundry Assembly Line</a>
      <a href="#kitchen-memory" class="primer-jump">&sect; 4. The Chef's Kitchen (Memory)</a>
      <a href="#vehicle-analogy" class="primer-jump">&sect; 5. The 4 Categories Explained</a>
      <a href="#benchmarking-101" class="primer-jump">&sect; 6. How We Score Chips</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="fundamentals-stream">
    <!-- SECTION 1: What is a Chip? -->
    <section id="what-is-a-chip" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">FIRST PRINCIPLES</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">What Exactly is a Computer Chip?</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        At the most fundamental physical level, a computer chip is made of pure refined <strong>sand (silicon)</strong> carved with billions of microscopic electrical switches called <strong>transistors</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="analogy-banner">
        <div class="analogy-icon">&#128161;</div>
        <div class="analogy-text">
          <strong>The Light Switch Analogy:</strong> Think of a transistor as a microscopic light switch. When electricity flows through, the switch is <strong>ON (1)</strong>. When blocked, it is <strong>OFF (0)</strong>. By connecting millions of these tiny switches together in clever patterns, we create <em>logic gates</em> that can add numbers, remember letters, and execute computer code!
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="fund-grid" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>Key Building Blocks</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bit (0 or 1):</strong> The smallest piece of information in computing.</li>
            <li><strong>Byte (8 bits):</strong> Enough bits to represent a single letter (like 'A' = <code>01000001</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Clock Cycle:</strong> The "heartbeat" or metronome of the chip. Every tick of the clock, the transistors update their state. A 1 GHz processor ticks <strong>one billion times every single second</strong>!</li>
            <li><strong>CPU (Central Processing Unit):</strong> The brain of the computer that reads instructions from memory and computes answers.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>From Code to Silicon</h4>
          <p>
            When you write code in Python, C++, or Java:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>A <strong>Compiler</strong> translates your readable English code into <strong>Assembly Instructions</strong> (simple commands like <code>ADD</code>, <code>SUB</code>, <code>LOAD</code>).</li>
            <li>The assembler turns those commands into binary <strong>Machine Code</strong> (raw 1s and 0s).</li>
            <li>The processor's transistors physically decode those 1s and 0s to route electrical currents and calculate results!</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 2: What is RISC-V? -->
    <section id="what-is-riscv" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">OPEN ARCHITECTURE</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">What is RISC-V and Why Does it Matter?</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        An <strong>Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)</strong> is the vocabulary or dictionary of commands that a processor understands. Without an ISA, software cannot communicate with hardware.
      </p>

      <div class="fund-grid">
        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>The Problem with Proprietary Chips</h4>
          <p>
            Historically, instruction sets were proprietary private property:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>x86 (Intel / AMD):</strong> Powers desktop PCs and servers. Proprietary and closed; no outside company or student can build an x86 chip without multi-million-dollar lawsuits.</li>
            <li><strong>ARM (Apple, Qualcomm, Raspberry Pi):</strong> Powers smartphones. Companies must pay millions in upfront licensing fees and ongoing royalties for every chip sold.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>The RISC-V Revolution: "The Linux of Silicon"</h4>
          <p>
            Invented at UC Berkeley in 2010, <strong>RISC-V</strong> (pronounced <em>"risk-five"</em>) is an <strong>open, royalty-free standard</strong>. Anyone—from high school students to research labs to global tech companies—can design, fabricate, and sell RISC-V chips without asking for permission or paying royalties!
          </p>
          <div class="highlight-callout">
            <strong>Modular LEGO-like Design:</strong> Start with a tiny integer base (<code>RV32I</code>), and snap on modular extensions like LEGO bricks: <code>M</code> (Multiply), <code>A</code> (Atomics), <code>C</code> (Compressed code), <code>F/D</code> (Floating-Point), and <code>V</code> (Vector acceleration).
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 3: The Laundry Assembly Line (Pipelining) -->
    <section id="pipeline-laundry" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">MICROARCHITECTURE</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">The Laundry Assembly Line: How Pipelining Works</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        Executing a single instruction requires multiple steps: <strong>Fetch</strong> the instruction from memory &rarr; <strong>Decode</strong> what it means &rarr; <strong>Execute</strong> the math &rarr; <strong>Access Memory</strong> &rarr; <strong>Write Back</strong> the answer. If a chip waited for one instruction to finish all 5 steps before starting the next, it would be painfully slow!
      </p>

      <div class="analogy-banner">
        <div class="analogy-icon">&#129530;</div>
        <div class="analogy-text">
          <strong>The Classic Laundry Analogy:</strong><br />
          Imagine doing 4 loads of laundry with a Washer (30 mins), a Dryer (30 mins), and Folding (30 mins):
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Without Pipelining:</strong> Wash load 1 &rarr; Dry load 1 &rarr; Fold load 1 (90 mins total). Then start load 2. Total time for 4 loads = <strong>360 minutes (6 hours)</strong>!</li>
            <li><strong>With Pipelining:</strong> As soon as load 1 moves into the dryer, throw load 2 into the washer! When load 1 is folding, load 2 is drying, and load 3 is washing. Total time for 4 loads = <strong>180 minutes (3 hours)</strong>! After the pipe fills, a new clean load finishes every 30 minutes!</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline-comparison-grid" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <div class="pipe-card">
          <span class="pipe-badge">1-STAGE / BIT-SERIAL</span>
          <h4>SERV (~2.1 kGE)</h4>
          <p>
            Does laundry <strong>one sock at a time</strong>. Processes exactly 1 bit per clock cycle over 32 cycles. Incredibly slow, but takes up virtually zero space (fits on the head of a pin).
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pipe-card">
          <span class="pipe-badge">2-STAGE PIPELINE</span>
          <h4>Ibex (~24.5 kGE)</h4>
          <p>
            Two stages: Fetch and Execute. Very fast branch recovery (if you guess wrong, you only waste 1 cycle). Perfect for secure smartcards and microcontrollers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pipe-card">
          <span class="pipe-badge">4-STAGE DUAL-ISSUE</span>
          <h4>VeeR EL2 (~48.5 kGE)</h4>
          <p>
            Two washers and two dryers! Fetches and executes <strong>two instructions simultaneously</strong> every clock cycle. High performance, but requires more wires and transistors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pipe-card">
          <span class="pipe-badge">6-STAGE APPLICATION</span>
          <h4>CVA6 (~120 kGE)</h4>
          <p>
            Deep 6-stage assembly line (Fetch, Decode, Issue, Execute, Commit, Writeback). Can run at high frequencies (> 1 GHz) and boot a full Linux operating system.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 4: The Chef's Kitchen (Memory Hierarchy) -->
    <section id="kitchen-memory" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">04</span>
        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">MEMORY SUBSYSTEMS</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">The Chef's Kitchen: Understanding the Memory Hierarchy</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        A fast processor without fast memory is like a world-class chef who has to drive across town every time they need a pinch of salt. Modern chips use a multi-tiered memory hierarchy to keep data right next to the computing core.
      </p>

      <div class="analogy-banner">
        <div class="analogy-icon">&#128104;&#8205;&#127859;</div>
        <div class="analogy-text">
          <strong>The Kitchen Counter Analogy:</strong>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Registers (Chef's Hands &amp; Cutting Board):</strong> Instant access (0 clock cycles). Extremely fast, but holds only 32 numbers!</li>
            <li><strong>L1 Cache (Kitchen Spice Rack):</strong> Inches away from the chef. Takes 1–2 clock cycles. Stores a few thousand bytes of frequently used ingredients.</li>
            <li><strong>Main Memory / DRAM (The Pantry Downstairs):</strong> Takes 50–200 clock cycles. Holds gigabytes of data, but the chef has to stop cooking and walk downstairs to retrieve items.</li>
            <li><strong>Storage / SSD / Flash (The Grocery Store):</strong> Takes thousands to millions of clock cycles! Holds terabytes permanently even when power is turned off.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="fund-grid" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>Scratchpad vs. Cache</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Tightly-Coupled Scratchpad (TCDM):</strong> Dedicated fast SRAM with guaranteed 1-cycle access time. The programmer controls exactly what is stored. Used in real-time embedded systems (Category 1) and manycore clusters (Category 4 <strong>MemPool</strong>).</li>
            <li><strong>Hardware Cache:</strong> Automatically guesses what data you'll need next. If it guesses right, it's a <em>Cache Hit</em> (fast). If it misses, it's a <em>Cache Miss</em> and the CPU must pause and wait.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>Virtual Memory &amp; The MMU (Memory Management Unit)</h4>
          <p>
            When you run multiple apps on your computer (like a browser and a game), how do they not overwrite each other's memory?
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The <strong>MMU</strong> gives every program its own private "virtual address book".</li>
            <li>A program thinks it has the whole memory to itself, while the MMU translates virtual addresses to actual physical RAM locations behind the scenes.</li>
            <li><strong>Linux requires an MMU</strong> (Category 2). Microcontrollers without an MMU run simpler "baremetal" code (Category 1).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 5: The 4 Categories via the Vehicle Analogy -->
    <section id="vehicle-analogy" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">05</span>
        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">THE BIG PICTURE</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">The 4 SCORE Categories: Explained via Vehicles</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        Why does SCORE categorize open-source chips into 4 categories? Because you wouldn't use a 50-car freight train to buy milk, nor could you haul 1,000 tons of steel on a bicycle. Different tasks demand fundamentally different chip architectures!
      </p>

      <div class="vehicle-grid">
        <div class="v-card cat1-v">
          <div class="v-icon">&#128692;</div>
          <span class="v-badge">CATEGORY 01</span>
          <h3>The Electric Bicycle</h3>
          <div class="v-subtitle">Microcontroller &amp; Scalar Cores</div>
          <p>
            Tiny, lightweight, highly efficient, and cheap. Ideal for simple tasks: checking battery levels, reading temperature sensors, and securing smartcards. Consumes microwatts of power.
          </p>
          <div class="v-examples">Examples: SERV, PicoRV32, Ibex, Hazard3</div>
          <a href="/mcu-scalar/" class="btn btn-outline btn-xs" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Explore Category 1 &rarr;</a>
        </div>

        <div class="v-card cat2-v">
          <div class="v-icon">&#128663;</div>
          <span class="v-badge">CATEGORY 02</span>
          <h3>The Passenger Sedan</h3>
          <div class="v-subtitle">64-bit Application Cores</div>
          <p>
            Comfortable, full-featured daily driver. Has an MMU, multi-level caches, and 64-bit addresses. Capable of running a full Linux operating system, web servers, and user software.
          </p>
          <div class="v-examples">Examples: CVA6 (Ariane), Rocket Chip, BOOM</div>
          <a href="/app-cores/" class="btn btn-outline btn-xs" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Explore Category 2 &rarr;</a>
        </div>

        <div class="v-card cat3-v">
          <div class="v-icon">&#128646;</div>
          <span class="v-badge">CATEGORY 03</span>
          <h3>The Heavy Cargo Train</h3>
          <div class="v-subtitle">Vector &amp; Coprocessor Engines</div>
          <p>
            Built for massive bulk data. Instead of computing 1 number at a time, its parallel lanes compute dozens of numbers in a single cycle. Perfect for AI matrix math, computer vision, and audio filters.
          </p>
          <div class="v-examples">Examples: Ara (RVV 1.0), AraXL, Gemmini</div>
          <a href="/vector-coprocessors/" class="btn btn-outline btn-xs" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Explore Category 3 &rarr;</a>
        </div>

        <div class="v-card cat4-v">
          <div class="v-icon">&#127970;</div>
          <span class="v-badge">CATEGORY 04</span>
          <h3>The Entire City Transit System</h3>
          <div class="v-subtitle">SoC Platforms &amp; Multi-Core Clusters</div>
          <p>
            Not just one vehicle, but a complete urban network! Contains highways (buses/NoC), traffic dispatchers (arbiters), autonomous delivery trucks (DMA engines), and hundreds of coordinated cores (up to 432 cores).
          </p>
          <div class="v-examples">Examples: MemPool (256 cores), Cheshire, Occamy</div>
          <a href="/soc-multicore/" class="btn btn-outline btn-xs" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Explore Category 4 &rarr;</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 6: How We Score Chips -->
    <section id="benchmarking-101" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">06</span>
        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">BENCHMARKING 101</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">How We Score &amp; Measure Chips</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        You can't just ask "which chip is the fastest?" You have to ask: <em>Fastest at what cost?</em> How much silicon area does it burn? How much energy does it consume? Here is how to read the scoreboard:
      </p>

      <div class="fund-grid">
        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>Efficiency &amp; Speed Metrics</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>CPI (Cycles per Instruction):</strong> Average clock ticks per instruction. Lower is better. A CPI of 1.0 means 1 instruction finishes every clock tick. Superscalar cores achieve CPI &lt; 1.0!
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>CoreMark / MHz:</strong> The standard SAT-exam for embedded processors. Measures how much real integer computing work the chip accomplishes for each tick of its clock.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Ops / Cycle:</strong> Operations completed per clock tick. Vector processors and systolic arrays achieve 4 to 30+ Ops/Cycle by computing numbers in parallel!
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Vector Utilization (%):</strong> How often the heavy math units are actually calculating numbers vs. sitting idle waiting for memory.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="fund-box">
          <h4>Physical Silicon Metrics</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Gate Count (kGE - Kilo Gate Equivalents):</strong> How many thousands of standard logic gates (NAND2 gates) the chip requires.
              <br />
              &bull; <code>SERV</code> = ~2.1 kGE (tiny)<br />
              &bull; <code>Ibex</code> = ~24.5 kGE (compact)<br />
              &bull; <code>CVA6</code> = ~120 kGE (medium application core)<br />
              &bull; <code>BOOM</code> = ~400+ kGE (large high-performance)
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Process Node (nm):</strong> The manufacturing technology (e.g. 130nm, 28nm, 16nm). Smaller nanometers allow packing more transistors into less space, running faster at lower voltages.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

